# Valgrind suppressions.
#
# Deliberately narrow. Each entry names a specific third-party allocation that
# the library cannot free and that is not evidence of a defect here; nothing
# in this file suppresses a frame belonging to libpsrp. The point of running
# memcheck at all is lost if the answer to an inconvenient finding is to
# silence it, so an entry needs a reason written next to it.

# OpenSSL builds its default provider and config state on first use and frees
# it from an atexit handler, which never runs under valgrind's accounting.
# The allocation has no frame of ours anywhere in it -- it is reached through
# libcurl and through gss-ntlmssp, both of which pull OpenSSL in on their own
# account. Calling OPENSSL_cleanup would silence it and is not an option for a
# library: it tears OpenSSL down for the entire process, including for a
# caller that is still using it.
{
   openssl-one-time-provider-state
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
}

# Same shape, reached through the Kerberos GSS mechanism's own initialisation.
{
   krb5-gss-one-time-state
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   obj:*/libgssapi_krb5.so.*
}
